WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.072
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.372 --> 00:00:08.572
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.872 --> 00:00:16.749
Unit twenty-two: Justice Across Generations. In this unit, you will debate our obligations to future generations.

4
00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:19.947
intergenerational justice.

5
00:00:20.647 --> 00:00:25.595
intergenerational justice means fairness between present and future generations.

6
00:00:25.895 --> 00:00:29.857
Climate change raises questions of intergenerational justice.

7
00:00:30.557 --> 00:00:34.092
Your turn. Say: intergenerational justice.

8
00:00:36.792 --> 00:00:38.942
intergenerational justice.

9
00:00:39.950 --> 00:00:41.543
future generations.

10
00:00:42.243 --> 00:00:45.996
future generations means people who will live after us.

11
00:00:46.296 --> 00:00:49.677
Our choices today will affect future generations.

12
00:00:50.377 --> 00:00:53.935
Your turn. Say: future generations.

13
00:00:56.635 --> 00:00:58.275
future generations.

14
00:00:59.283 --> 00:01:00.633
long-termism.

15
00:01:01.333 --> 00:01:08.986
long-termism means the view that positively influencing the long-term future is a key priority.

16
00:01:09.286 --> 00:01:12.505
Long-termism shapes some philanthropic decisions.

17
00:01:13.205 --> 00:01:16.438
Your turn. Say: long-termism.

18
00:01:19.138 --> 00:01:20.336
long-termism.

19
00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:23.779
sustainability ethic.

20
00:01:24.479 --> 00:01:30.507
sustainability ethic means a moral commitment to meeting present needs without harming the future.

21
00:01:30.807 --> 00:01:34.235
A sustainability ethic guides the company's choices.

22
00:01:34.935 --> 00:01:38.516
Your turn. Say: sustainability ethic.

23
00:01:41.216 --> 00:01:42.995
sustainability ethic.

24
00:01:43.983 --> 00:01:45.588
generational fairness.

25
00:01:46.288 --> 00:01:50.761
generational fairness means equal treatment of different age groups.

26
00:01:51.061 --> 00:01:55.092
Pension reforms raise issues of generational fairness.

27
00:01:55.792 --> 00:01:59.036
Your turn. Say: generational fairness.

28
00:02:01.736 --> 00:02:03.457
generational fairness.

29
00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:05.869
inheritance of debt.

30
00:02:06.569 --> 00:02:11.819
inheritance of debt means passing public or private debts to the next generation.

31
00:02:12.119 --> 00:02:14.839
Young people worry about the inheritance of debt.

32
00:02:15.539 --> 00:02:19.097
Your turn. Say: inheritance of debt.

33
00:02:21.797 --> 00:02:23.262
inheritance of debt.

34
00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:25.715
climate legacy.

35
00:02:26.415 --> 00:02:31.468
climate legacy means the climate impacts we leave to future generations.

36
00:02:31.768 --> 00:02:34.952
Our climate legacy will last for centuries.

37
00:02:35.652 --> 00:02:38.955
Your turn. Say: climate legacy.

38
00:02:41.655 --> 00:02:42.911
climate legacy.

39
00:02:43.917 --> 00:02:45.254
temporal justice.

40
00:02:45.954 --> 00:02:50.531
temporal justice means fairness across different periods of time.

41
00:02:50.831 --> 00:02:55.629
Temporal justice asks whether the future counts as much as the present.

42
00:02:56.329 --> 00:02:59.283
Your turn. Say: temporal justice.

43
00:03:01.983 --> 00:03:03.333
temporal justice.

44
00:03:04.317 --> 00:03:06.560
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:10.729
Ms. Sarah, do we owe anything to people who don't exist yet?

46
00:03:12.017 --> 00:03:15.862
Most philosophers would argue that we do, at least to some extent.

47
00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:19.266
Even if we can't know their preferences?

48
00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:25.591
Arguably, certain needs, such as clean air and water, are fairly predictable.

49
00:03:26.883 --> 00:03:29.800
Long-termism goes further, doesn't it?

50
00:03:31.117 --> 00:03:36.335
It does. Its critics contend that it may neglect urgent present suffering.

51
00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:39.417
And our climate legacy?

52
00:03:40.717 --> 00:03:47.720
It would seem difficult to defend leaving future generations both a damaged climate and an inheritance of debt.

53
00:03:49.017 --> 00:03:53.210
Grammar focus: Stance in academic writing (extended).

54
00:03:53.510 --> 00:03:56.450
Academic stance can be firm yet polite.

55
00:03:56.750 --> 00:04:03.466
We owe them something, at least to some extent. Its critics contend that it may neglect present suffering.

56
00:04:03.766 --> 00:04:06.451
It would seem difficult to defend such a legacy.

57
00:04:06.751 --> 00:04:13.592
Strong academic writers can make a firm point politely: 'It would seem difficult to defend ...' is firmer than it looks.

58
00:04:14.583 --> 00:04:17.572
Now read and listen. Our Debt to the Future.

59
00:04:17.872 --> 00:04:22.519
Every generation inherits a world shaped by those who came before.

60
00:04:23.817 --> 00:04:28.986
The question of intergenerational justice asks what we owe to those who will come after us.

61
00:04:30.283 --> 00:04:36.404
Some philosophers argue that future generations have the same moral status as people alive today.

62
00:04:37.717 --> 00:04:44.465
Long-termism takes this further, prioritising actions that could benefit billions of future lives.

63
00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:51.221
Its critics contend that such reasoning may justify neglecting urgent present needs.

64
00:04:52.517 --> 00:04:59.311
Practical issues, such as the inheritance of public debt and our climate legacy, make the debate concrete.

65
00:05:00.617 --> 00:05:06.982
A sustainability ethic attempts to balance present welfare with long-term responsibility.

66
00:05:08.283 --> 00:05:13.499
It would seem difficult, at any rate, to justify leaving the future poorer than we found it.

67
00:05:14.783 --> 00:05:17.424
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:17.724 --> 00:05:21.825
Fairness between present and future generations is blank.

69
00:05:22.125 --> 00:05:28.402
A, intergenerational justice. B, long-termism. C, inheritance of debt.

70
00:05:32.417 --> 00:05:36.030
The answer is A: intergenerational justice.

71
00:05:37.517 --> 00:05:39.191
Which phrase limits a claim?

72
00:05:39.491 --> 00:05:44.073
A, to some extent. B, definitely. C, always.

73
00:05:48.083 --> 00:05:50.919
The answer is A: to some extent.

74
00:05:52.417 --> 00:05:55.568
'Its critics blank that the policy is unfair.'

75
00:05:55.868 --> 00:06:00.032
A, contend. B, tell. C, speak.

76
00:06:04.017 --> 00:06:06.353
The answer is A: contend.

77
00:06:07.850 --> 00:06:11.603
The climate impacts we leave to the future are our blank.

78
00:06:11.903 --> 00:06:17.623
A, climate legacy. B, temporal justice. C, generational fairness.

79
00:06:21.617 --> 00:06:24.255
The answer is A: climate legacy.

80
00:06:25.750 --> 00:06:29.317
According to the critics, what might long-termism neglect?

81
00:06:29.617 --> 00:06:35.186
A, future lives. B, urgent present needs. C, the climate.

82
00:06:39.183 --> 00:06:42.518
The answer is B: urgent present needs.

83
00:06:44.017 --> 00:06:48.654
Now it's your turn. Part one: What do we owe to future generations?

84
00:06:53.954 --> 00:06:59.727
Part two: Use stance markers; identify two obligations and one limit.

85
00:07:06.027 --> 00:07:20.791
We owe them, at least, a livable climate and manageable public debt. It would seem difficult to defend anything less. That said, our obligations to future people should not, I think, override urgent duties to those suffering today.

86
00:07:21.091 --> 00:07:23.822
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:24.817 --> 00:07:30.592
Great work today! You learned eight new words and stance in academic writing (extended).

88
00:07:30.892 --> 00:07:35.147
Today's value is responsibility. Help at home and do your jobs.

89
00:07:35.447 --> 00:07:38.993
See you in Unit twenty-three. Goodbye!

90
00:07:39.293 --> 00:07:41.609
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

